  - The Agent-to-Agent (A2A) protocol specification version 1.0, released by Google in early 2026 [ev-15], fundamentally reorients AI communication from a strictly synchronous request-response model toward an architecture where long-running asynchronous work is treated as a first-class citizen. [Source: ev-18]
  - Unlike the Model Context Protocol (MCP), which primarily facilitates connections between agents and external tools via stdio or HTTP with limited native support for complex lifecycles [ev-40], A2A natively supports three distinct communication patterns: immediate synchronous requests, streaming updates via Server-Sent Events (SSE), and persistent push notifications through webhooks [ev-31]. [Source: ev-18]
  - This tripartite approach allows a single implementation to adapt its transport layer dynamically; while JSON-RPC 2.0 over HTTP serves quick queries, SSE handles incremental data delivery such as research findings, and webhook-based subscriptions manage operations that cannot block the client thread without degrading performance [ev-17]. [Source: ev-18]
